,"html_url":"https://github.com/narrowstacks/dorkroom/commit/53efda54b276e443ec38b7130272d50ac24673ab","node_id":"C_kwDOP0YsJdoAKDUzZWZkYTU0YjI3NmU0NDNlYzM4YjcxMzAyNzJkNTBhYzI0NjczYWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/narrowstacks/dorkroom/commit/80d9b70b417b56d3b6e853a83a78768d703dd87b","sha":"80d9b70b417b56d3b6e853a83a78768d703dd87b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/narrowstacks/dorkroom/commits/80d9b70b417b56d3b6e853a83a78768d703dd87b"}],"sha":"53efda54b276e443ec38b7130272d50ac24673ab","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/narrowstacks/dorkroom/commits/53efda54b276e443ec38b7130272d50ac24673ab"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2106980?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/narrowstacks","id":2106980,"login":"narrowstacks","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxMDY5ODA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/narrowstacks","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/narrowstacks/dorkroom/commits/80d9b70b417b56d3b6e853a83a78768d703dd87b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-04T23:47:59Z","email":"aaron@affords.art","name":"aaron f.a"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-04T23:47:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(license): AGPLv3 §7 App Store exception, and MIT for @dorkroom/api (#283)\n\nTwo licensing fixes, plus the review follow-up.\n\nApp Store exception. Apple conditions App Store distribution on end users\naccepting terms that restrict rights the AGPLv3 grants (device-count limits,\nDRM on the delivered binary). AGPLv3 §12 says that when you cannot satisfy the\nlicense and other pertinent obligations simultaneously, you may not convey the\nwork at all. That is what got VLC pulled from the App Store in 2011, and\napps/mobile was headed for the same wall. LICENSE-EXCEPTION grants an additional\npermission under §7 waiving only the conditions that collide with store terms\n(§6 Installation Information, §10 further restrictions, §12 conflicting\nobligations), and only to the extent the store makes compliance impossible.\nCopyleft and source availability are untouched and restated explicitly: a\nmodified build shipped through a store is still AGPLv3 and still owes its\nCorresponding Source. §7 also requires the added terms to be pointed to from the\nrelevant source files, so each covered work carries the notice at its entry\npoint: apps/mobile/index.js, apps/dorkroom/src/main.tsx,\npackages/logic/src/index.ts, packages/ui/src/index.ts. LICENSE itself is\nuntouched; the FSF text stays verbatim.\n\nGranting it now matters: the copyright is currently held by one person, so the\npermission can be given unilaterally. Once an outside contributor lands a patch\nin packages/logic, which the iOS app consumes, that stops being true.\n\n@dorkroom/api relicensed to MIT. The package is set up for npm publication and\nis a client for a public REST API, so AGPL made it unusable for its entire\naudience: importing it would pull the consumer''s whole application into the\ncopyleft. It has no runtime workspace dependencies and imports nothing\nAGPL-licensed, so the relicense needed no third-party consent. NOTICE point 5\nnow states this as a grant rather than leaving it to be inferred.\n\nCONTRIBUTING states inbound per package, matching each file''s outbound license:\nMIT for packages/api, AGPL-3.0 plus the exception everywhere else. Without the\ncarve-out the first outside API patch would have recreated the relicensing\nblocker this change removes. No CLA, no copyright assignment, no proprietary\nrelicensing right.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CBtoEroHwn3orGesticYN8","tree":{"sha":"c19c6f725a93712574d6f82fb6cec80e86a6ad90","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/narrowstacks/dorkroom/git/trees/c19c6f725a93712574d6f82fb6cec80e86a6ad90"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/narrowstacks/dorkroom/git/commits/80d9b70b417b56d3b6e853a83a78768d703dd87b","verification":{"payload":"tree c19c6f725a93712574d6f82fb6cec80e86a6ad90\nparent f0be401b202bd5c86f21060949a3880cbef39b9e\nauthor aaron f.a <aaron@affords.art> 1788565679 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788565679 -0700\n\ndocs(license): AGPLv3 §7 App Store exception, and MIT for @dorkroom/api (#283)\n\nTwo licensing fixes, plus the review follow-up.\n\nApp Store exception. Apple conditions App Store distribution on end users\naccepting terms that restrict rights the AGPLv3 grants (device-count limits,\nDRM on the delivered binary). AGPLv3 §12 says that when you cannot satisfy the\nlicense and other pertinent obligations simultaneously, you may not convey the\nwork at all. That is what got VLC pulled from the App Store in 2011, and\napps/mobile was headed for the same wall. LICENSE-EXCEPTION grants an additional\npermission under §7 waiving only the conditions that collide with store terms\n(§6 Installation Information, §10 further restrictions, §12 conflicting\nobligations), and only to the extent the store makes compliance impossible.\nCopyleft and source availability are untouched and restated explicitly: a\nmodified build shipped through a store is still AGPLv3 and still owes its\nCorresponding Source. §7 also requires the added terms to be pointed to from the\nrelevant source files, so each covered work carries the notice at its entry\npoint: apps/mobile/index.js, apps/dorkroom/src/main.tsx,\npackages/logic/src/index.ts, packages/ui/src/index.ts. LICENSE itself is\nuntouched; the FSF text stays verbatim.\n\nGranting it now matters: the copyright is currently held by one person, so the\npermission can be given unilaterally. Once an outside contributor lands a patch\nin packages/logic, which the iOS app consumes, that stops being true.\n\n@dorkroom/api relicensed to MIT. The package is set up for npm publication and\nis a client for a public REST API, so AGPL made it unusable for its entire\naudience: importing it would pull the consumer''s whole application into the\ncopyleft. It has no runtime workspace dependencies and imports nothing\nAGPL-licensed, so the relicense needed no third-party consent. NOTICE point 5\nnow states this as a grant rather than leaving it to be inferred.\n\nCONTRIBUTING states inbound per package, matching each file''s outbound license:\nMIT for packages/api, AGPL-3.0 plus the exception everywhere else. Without the\ncarve-out the first outside API patch would have recreated the relicensing\nblocker this change removes. No CLA, no copyright assignment, no proprietary\nrelicensing right.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CBtoEroHwn3orGesticYN8","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqm1ivCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAJ+oQAHYjo1+fnZ6OsJmZHcSC79up\n22Wwq+4vZRrXxXbBQmiyJaGT16586lUbjWycUh8bYE9z9UMmOrjKpuH0zFnYgYWw\nvlMMr2PJFIpUEaUqqWQyQmQd3w4alngckiAdVNFiKtcCjRFnMNUvprllYp8RJQWw\nUow/zx0QGe0XK6p523jkMcdEMYzm9mRTh9/QDi6+892Qr7FYYkkAK0mSy8Z3bTYb\nA5+h4DJsIdArRDVZ4WlW7DDyq/u+es2suKPCXDK5TnwHqS2lxpa7/v/HMQh56Z94\natFGCIMv7WmAanl/8B8vZecSieeNMEcwdviXLbBabzTcg1gecPBjsNaPMZOt1eCp\nQD2ONuqCSaeTcmIhcBrcxdVXpR8oSsPl2BtGTVSbkf0mqz117VcHA/P9nvsq5hmv\nBVYS7n3vdE/fCuJqx0itRmnGyzwpLEzIo4JlVn1+sgBtc7a8wBbUgTorGVYUQ3+S\nqG80Te9